course: chemistry
lesson: 24
title: Soap and why washing works
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: After pizza your fingers are greasy, and rinsing under the tap does almost nothing: water and grease do not mix. Add soap and the grease slides away. Each soap particle has a water-loving head and a grease-loving tail; the tails grab the grease and the heads pull it into the water.
explain: Soap also breaks apart many germs, which is why washing your hands properly, for about 20 seconds, or two rounds of "Happy Birthday", helps stop illness.
explain: Soap weakens the "skin" of water, its surface tension. Sprinkle pepper on water, touch it with a soapy finger, and the pepper races to the edges, pulled by the stronger skin there. Soapy water also makes a stretchy film that you can blow into bubbles, and a free bubble always pulls itself into a ball.
explain: Long ago people made soap by boiling fat with lye from wood ash, a very harsh alkali, so not a kitchen experiment. Today washing powders also carry enzymes that break down food stains, and hand gel uses alcohol to kill germs, though it does not lift dirt.
fact: Each soap particle has a water-loving head and an oil-loving tail; the tails grab grease and the heads pull it into the water.
fact: Soap breaks apart some germs, so handwashing helps stop illness; washing takes about 20 seconds, the time of two rounds of "Happy Birthday".
fact: Soap weakens the surface tension of water; the stronger "skin" at the edges pulls pepper away from a soapy finger, and a paper boat with soap at the back zooms forward for the same reason.
fact: Soap makes the water's skin flexible so it does not snap, which is why plain water cannot be blown into bubbles; a little glycerine or sugar makes bubbles last longer.
fact: A free bubble always becomes a sphere, because a sphere holds the air inside with the smallest surface; even square wire frames make round bubbles.
fact: In hard-water areas, minerals in the water react with soap to make grey scum, and kettles get white crusts of limescale.
fact: In history, soap was made by boiling fat with lye from wood ash; the alkali breaks fats into soap, and lye is very caustic.
fact: Washing powder contains detergents and enzymes that break down protein and fat stains, so many modern powders work well at 30 °C and save energy.
fact: Foam is just lots of bubbles; soap particles do the cleaning whether there is foam or not.
fact: Dishwasher detergent is made not to foam, so washing-up liquid must never go in a dishwasher: the foam would overflow.
fact: Alcohol hand gel kills many germs by damaging their outer coat, but it does not remove dirt, works poorly on dirty hands, is flammable and must not be swallowed.
exercise: How can soap stick to both water and grease? | Its particles have a water-loving head and grease-loving tail | It turns all the grease straight into clean water | It is half oil and half water mixed together in a bar | It melts grease with heat
exercise: Why can't water alone clean greasy fingers? | Water and grease don't mix | Water is too cold to work | Grease is heavier than water | Water makes grease stickier
exercise: How does soap help stop illness? | It breaks apart germs and washes them off | It makes the germs fall fast asleep for a while | It makes the skin too slippery for germs | It cools the hands so germs die
exercise: About how long should you wash your hands? | About 20 seconds | About 2 seconds | About 2 minutes | About 10 minutes
exercise: Why does pepper run away from a drop of soap on water? | Soap weakens the skin there and the stronger edges pull it | The soap pushes the pepper with a puff of air | Pepper is scared of the soap's smell | The soap makes the water flow outwards in a wave
exercise: Why does a paper boat with soap at the back zoom along? | The water's skin pulls harder at the front | The soap fizzes behind it like a tiny engine | The soap makes the boat lighter | The soap melts the water behind it
exercise: Why can't you blow bubbles with plain water? | Its skin snaps instead of stretching | It is far too heavy to float in the air | It has no air in it | It is too warm to hold a shape
exercise: What makes soap bubbles last longer? | A little glycerine or sugar | A big pinch of table salt | More cold water | A drop of vinegar
exercise: Why are bubbles never square? | A sphere holds the air with the smallest surface | Bubble mixture is always round inside the bottle | The wind rounds off the corners | Square bubbles are much too heavy to float up
exercise: What shape bubble comes out of a square wire frame? | A round one | A square one | A triangle | A cube
exercise: What makes the grey scum in a bath in some towns? | Minerals in hard water reacting with soap | Dirt and grime that falls off the old bath tiles | Too much warm water | Bubbles that dried in the air
exercise: What is the white crust inside kettles in hard-water towns? | Limescale from minerals in the water | Soap left over from all the washing up | Salt from the sea | Old milk from tea
exercise: How did people make soap before shops? | Boiled fat with lye from wood ash | Mixed sugar with lemon juice and honey | Dried seawater in the sun | Ground up white stones
exercise: Why can modern laundry be washed at 30 °C? | Enzymes in the powder break down stains | Clothes are cleaner than they used to be | Cold water has more soap in it | Machines now spin much faster
exercise: Does foam do the cleaning in washing-up? | No, soap particles clean with or without it | Yes, only the foam cleans | Yes, the more foam there is, the cleaner the plates | No, foam makes plates dirtier
exercise: Why mustn't you put washing-up liquid in a dishwasher? | Its foam would overflow | It would melt the plates | It cannot reach the plates | It would turn the water blue
exercise: When is soap better than hand gel? | When your hands are dirty | When your hands look clean | Only after eating sweets | Never, gel is always better
test: "i just splashed my hands under the tap after the garden, that's fine yeah?" What's true? | Soap and about 20 seconds work much better | Yes, water alone washes away every single germ | Yes, if the water is really cold | No, you need to scrub with sand
test: Mia uses gel on muddy hands and says they're clean now. What's the catch? | Gel doesn't remove dirt and works poorly on dirty hands | Gel turns mud into soap | Gel only works on wet hands | Nothing, gel is better than soap here
test: Little cousin is about to put washing-up liquid in the dishwasher "to make it cleaner". What happens? | Foam overflows out of the machine | The dishes come out extra clean and shiny | Nothing happens at all | The machine works twice as fast
test: A cartoon shows a cube-shaped soap bubble floating across the sky. What would a real bubble do? | Pull itself into a ball | Keep its cube shape | Turn into a triangle | Burst before it can form
test: Josh says lots of foam means the washing-up liquid is working harder. What's true? | The soap cleans whether there's foam or not | He's right, foam does the cleaning | Foam is just water and air with no soap | More foam makes plates greasier
test: If you put a drop of soap behind a paper boat on a still pond, what would happen? | The boat would shoot forward | The boat would sink at once | Nothing would happen at all | The boat would spin in circles
test: "my bubbles keep popping straight away". What could help? | Add a little glycerine or sugar | Use only plain cold tap water | Add a spoon of salt | Blow much, much harder and faster
test: Grandma's new town makes grey scum in the bath and crusty kettles. What's going on? | The water is hard, full of minerals | The pipes are full of old soap from before | The water is too soft | Someone put salt in the water tank
test: "ugh old-timey soap from wood ash, let's make some in the kitchen!" What's the honest reply? | No, lye is very caustic, not for kids | Sure, it's just warm water and ash | Sure, if you add lemon juice | Yes, but only in a plastic bowl
test: Why can washing powder get an egg stain out even at 30 °C? | Its enzymes break down the stain | The egg dissolves in any water | The machine boils the water anyway | The stain fades in the dark drum
test: Hand gel left by the birthday candles. What's the problem? | It is flammable | It will melt into soap | It turns the cake sour | Nothing, it's only water
exam: Identify the feature of the soap particle by virtue of which it removes grease. | It possesses a water-attracting head and a grease-attracting tail | It is entirely composed of water | It dissolves grease by the application of considerable heat to the surface | It converts grease directly into gas
exam: It is given that a free soap bubble invariably assumes a spherical form. Ascertain the principal reason. | A sphere encloses the air with the smallest surface | The wind removes the corners of other shapes | The mixture is spherical within its container | The air within is heavier at the centre
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises alcohol hand gel? | It kills many germs but does not remove dirt, and it is flammable | It removes dirt more effectively than soap and water | It is safe to swallow in small quantities | It functions best upon visibly dirty hands
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning foam in washing-up. | Cleaning is performed by the soap particles irrespective of foam | Cleaning is performed exclusively by the foam | The greater the foam, the cleaner the dishes, invariably | Foam is required for any cleaning whatsoever
